the dow jones industrial average just  hit 30k  for the first time since its inception  in 1896.  after a long rally the dow surged to  this milestone as investors cheered  breakthroughs in three separate covit 19  vaccines  offering hope the economy may soon get  back to normal  it also followed democrat joe biden's  victory in the presidential election  and the general services administration  clearing the way for the  president-elect's transition process  easing investors concerns about a  drawn-out period of uncertainty  this latest stock market rise was driven  by stimulus programs from the fed  and leadership from tech stocks the  speed of the climb from twenty thousand  to thirty thousand has still been  astounding despite  what we saw earlier in the year which  was a huge sell-off in february and  march  it took about 18 years for the dow to  get from 10 000 to 20 000  and it's been less than four years of  the dow to climb from 20 000 to 30  thousand  the new high comes around nine months  after the dow plummeted from investors  fearing the economic impacts of the  coronavirus  during this period of historic  volatility on several occasions the dow  fell  more than three percent in a single day  after reaching its february 12  peak the blue chip index fell nearly 40  percent in just six weeks  hitting its lowest closing level since  2016.  when it comes to the dow milestones like  30 000 serve  as an indicator of how well shares of a  select group of large  technology companies have done and in  that regard the biggest contributor to  the dow's rally  apple underscores the influence of the  tech sector on the global economy during  the pandemic  more on the dow's other leaders and  laggards in a moment  but first let's take a look at how the  index works  the dow looks a lot different today than  it did in 1896 when it was first created  with 12  smokestack companies so these were  companies like tennessee coal and iron  general electric  that were old economy companies today  the index has 30 stocks that are  weighted by stock price  and we've seen tech companies like apple  and microsoft and more recently  salesforce really have a big influence  editors at the wall street journal help  select the 30 companies that make up the  dow  those stocks are generally seen by  investors as the pillars of the stock  market  when it comes to the dow the more  expensive the stock the more  polar company has so a two percent stock  price gained by  united health would add more points to  the dow than say  a two percent gain by cisco its stock  price is a fraction of united health  other stock indexes like the s p 500 are  very different from the dow because they  weight companies by market  capitalization so companies with the  biggest market values like apple will  have a much bigger influence on the s p  500  than they do on the dow that means just  a handful of stocks have driven up the  value of the dow over the last few years  take boeing for example before two  deadly crashes the grounding of the 737  max  fleet and the pandemic the aerospace  company was driving the dow's gains  more recently tech companies like apple  and microsoft have taken the baton from  boeing and  overshadowed the declines in shares of  former market leaders exxon mobil  ibm and ge while recently the  coronavirus crisis has been a driving  factor in the success of certain  companies  and the travails of others political  factors have long played a critical role  in moving markets  the are paying a fair share of taxes  even as the stock market shatters  records our stock market has reached an  all-time high today 2017 was a pretty  good year for the stock market  the world's major economies were growing  in sync corporate profits were rising  and the dow continued to steadily climb  higher  in november the release of the gop's tax  cut plan would have a direct impact on  those 30 companies that make up the dow  with this plan we are making pro growth  reforms  so that yes america can compete with the  rest of the world  investors consider the tax cuts good for  big business  the dow is moved day-to-day by the same  factors that influenced the broader  stock market  these could be economic indicators  political questions  or recently momentum signals 2018  was full of uncertainty there were trade  tensions with china  that threatened globalized businesses  bottom line in particular with china  we're going to be doing a section  301 trade action  it could be about 60 billion dollars  but that's really just a fraction of  what we're talking about  and president trump criticized the  federal reserve for a dip  in the markets i think the fed has gone  crazy  in 2019 the fed cut interest rates for  the first time since the financial  crisis  not just once but three times today we  decided to lower the interest rates for  the third time this year  other central banks around the world  took actions to stabilize their  economies as well  and towards the end of 2019 some  progress was made after months of trade  talks between the us and china  president trump agreed to a limited deal  with beijing  dropping threatened tariffs as china  agreed to purchase more goods from the  u.s  by january of 2020 the president had  signed the first phase of a trade  agreement  and the dow in the early months of the  year with gdp and unemployment numbers  strong  continued its steady march toward 30k in  mid-february  amidst the profound economic  uncertainties of a global pandemic  and the widespread shutdowns in march  the dao  like all major world indices witnessed a  historic sell-off  but then from early april onward  investors found hope  in multi-trillion dollar federal relief  packages  the federal reserve's aggressive  stimulus programs and growing optimism  that a vaccine is on the way  in addition to apple shares of microsoft  amazon  google parent alphabet and facebook have  soared as the pandemic and trends like  remote working  and learning bolster consumers reliance  on new technology  for the s p 500 and the tech laden  nasdaq  these companies have more than  compensated for the declines in  economically sensitive sectors like  energy  while many analysts are wary of a  reversal following such a strong rally  investors say the fundamental drivers of  the run are still intact  particularly for tech companies whose  growth is even more appealing in a world  with  ultra low interest rates who knows where  stocks will go from here the coronavirus  has  really introduced a whole new level of  uncertainty in both directions  investors have really been staggered by  how quickly stocks fell  earlier in the year and how quickly they  since recovered and how fast the dow is  now at 30k  so who knows each 10 000 points gets  easier so  40k might not be out of the question
 

